<description>
Recolor the concentric rectangular rings by cycling the outside-to-inside color order one step outward.

Input structure:
- The whole grid is partitioned into one-cell-thick concentric rectangular rings.
- Each ring is monochromatic.
- Reading rings from outside to inside gives at least two distinct colors; a color may reappear on a later ring.

Rule:
- Record the distinct colors in their first outside-to-inside ring order.
- Replace every pixel of each recorded color with the previous color in that cyclic order.

Key insight:
- The transformation is a color-cycle over nesting order, not over numeric color ids or ring sizes.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "concentric monochrome rectangular rings, read from outside inward",
  "transform": "record distinct ring colors in first outside-in order; cyclically recolor every pixel of each color to the previous color in that order"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: even square, 6-10 rows/cols (i.e. 3-5 rings)
  rings:
    structure: concentric 1-cell-thick rectangular rings filling the entire grid; each ring is monochromatic
    distinct_colors: 3 (exactly); picked from ARC palette 0-9, all different
    color_sequence: outside-in ring colors follow pattern where the first repetition may occur on ring index = num_distinct (e.g. 4 rings -> [A,B,C,A])
  transform:
    description: record distinct colors in first outside-in appearance order [A,B,C]; cyclically recolor A->C, B->A, C->B (each color maps to the previous in the cyclic list)
    output: same grid dimensions and ring structure, only colors change

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (task is simple enough)
  steps:
    1. pick grid size N from {6, 8, 10} (even square)
    2. pick 3 distinct colors from 0-9
    3. compute num_rings = N/2; assign ring colors outside-in repeating the 3-color sequence cyclically (ring i gets color[i % 3])
    4. fill each ring with its assigned color to produce the input grid
    5. derive output by applying the cyclic mapping A->C, B->A, C->B
```
</generation_spec>
